{
  "dom_id": "step-q40333",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given the polynomial $P(x)=-{x}^{2}(x+1)(x-2)(x+2),$ find the solution of $P(x)\\le 0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)\\le 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=-{x}^{2}(x+1)(x-2)(x+2)$ are $x=\\pm 2,x=-1$ and $x=0,$ where\n\n- $x=\\pm 2$ and $x=-1$ are simple roots, and\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a quintic polynomial with a negative leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033945/assets/1fb947e6360139669bae0821e0de8460da53ead628f589998fab1ce442646cdf.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)\\le 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in [-2,-1]\\cup {0}\\cup [2,\\infty ).$",
